=== blocked: bare scalar in a for-list
exit 2
--- stderr
[bendit] BLOCKED: `for ... in $files` runs ONCE under zsh, not once per word.
This shell is zsh, which does not word-split a bare parameter. The loop
body would run on the whole string, or not at all if it is empty -- and
the command would still exit 0, so whatever it checks would report success.

Use one of:
  bash -c '...'                      run the loop in bash, which splits
  for x in $(printf %s "$files"); do   command substitution DOES split
  while IFS= read -r x; do ... done <<< "$files"   ONE PER LINE only
  for x in "${arr[@]}"               if it is already an array

The read loop splits on NEWLINES, not spaces: right for `git ls-files`
output, wrong for a space-separated string, where it gives one iteration
and reproduces this bug quietly. Measured, not assumed.

If $files really is a zsh array, this is a false positive: say so and run
it under bash -c, or quote the expansion to show the single-item intent.
(Disable this guard with the plugin option zshGuard=false.)

